---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { pages } from '../lib/pages';
---

<Base title="How tdfa-jvm works">
    <header class="mb-12">
        <div class="mb-3 font-mono text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest text-teal-700">TDFA-JVM EXPLAINED</div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 sm:text-5xl">
            How a regex engine<br /><span class="text-teal-700">can match in linear time</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">
            <a href="https://github.com/jemmix/tdfa-jvm" class="font-medium text-teal-700 hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tdfa-jvm</a>{' '}
            compiles every regular expression into a deterministic finite automaton — and then into JVM bytecode. Matching is a table walk: one step per input character, for every pattern the parser accepts.
            This site explains how that pipeline works, stage by stage, so that you can read the code, extend it, or reimplement it — if hash maps and BFS are in your toolbox, this is for you; no automata-theory background assumed.
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-bold text-zinc-900">The one-minute version</h2>
        <div class="space-y-3">
            {[
                ['Parse', 'the pattern text becomes a syntax tree; every capture group gets two "tags" — integers marking its open and close boundaries.'],
                ['Thompson construction', 'the tree becomes a TNFA: a graph whose "free move" edges carry those tags plus priorities, so the graph itself encodes both <i>what to capture</i> and <i>which alternative is preferred</i>.'],
                ['Determinize', 'subset construction folds every possible "thread list" into DFA states. The tag bookkeeping that would be unbounded gets resolved <i>at compile time</i> into tiny register operations — SET position, COPY, SET nil — attached to the DFA transitions.'],
                ['Optimize & minimize', 'a register-allocation pass (the same ideas a compiler uses for CPU registers) shrinks the register file; a Moore-algorithm pass merges redundant states.'],
                ['Execute', 'the automaton runs as a flat int-array walk — or as generated JVM bytecode, one <span class="mono">tableswitch</span> per state. Unanchored search adds a scan tier that finds leftmost starts without restarting the walk at every offset.'],
            ].map(([t, d]) => (
                <div class="flex gap-4 rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-4">
                    <div class="w-28 shrink-0 pt-0.5 text-right font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-teal-700">{t}</div>
                    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600" set:html={d} />
                </div>
            ))}
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-500">
            The trade-off, stated plainly: match time is linear in input length regardless of pattern (there is no backtracking engine in the library at all), while compilation is slower than <span class="mono">java.util.regex</span> and backreferences and lookaround are not supported. On the benchmark corpus this comes out to 2–6× faster than re2j on short-input search, with some literal-heavy scenarios still won by <span class="mono">java.util.regex</span> — numbers and known gaps are tracked in <span class="mono">BENCHMARKS.md</span>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-bold text-zinc-900">The pipeline, end to end</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 p-5">
            <div class="flex min-w-max items-stretch gap-2">
                {[
                    { artifact: '"(\\w+)@(\\w+)"', stage: 'Parser', cls: 'parser/Parser.java', out: 'AST + tags t1..t4', href: '/parsing/' },
                    { artifact: 'AST', stage: 'Tnfa.Builder', cls: 'tnfa/Tnfa.java', out: 'TNFA (tagged NFA)', href: '/tnfa/' },
                    { artifact: 'TNFA', stage: 'TdfaCompiler', cls: 'tdfa/TdfaCompiler.java', out: 'DeterminizedDfa', href: '/determinization/' },
                    { artifact: 'DeterminizedDfa', stage: 'TdfaMaterializer', cls: 'tdfa/TdfaMaterializer.java', out: 'Tdfa (flat tables)', href: '/registers/' },
                    { artifact: 'Tdfa', stage: 'ASM / VM', cls: 'asm/TdfaAsmBackend.java', out: 'bytecode or interpreter', href: '/asm/' },
                    { artifact: 'input text', stage: 'TdfaRunner', cls: 'tdfa/TdfaRunner.java', out: 'MatchResult', href: '/execution/' },
                ].map((s, i) => (
                    <div class="flex items-stretch gap-2">
                        {i > 0 && <div class="flex items-center text-zinc-300">→</div>}
                        <a href={s.href} class="block w-44 rounded-lg border border-zinc-300 bg-white p-3 transition hover:border-teal-500 hover:shadow-sm">
                            <div class="mono mb-1 truncate text-[10px] text-zinc-400">{s.artifact}</div>
                            <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-800">{s.stage}</div>
                            <div class="mono mt-0.5 truncate text-[10px] text-zinc-400">{s.cls}</div>
                            <div class="mono mt-2 text-[11px] text-teal-700">↓ {s.out}</div>
                        </a>
                    </div>
                ))}
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-zinc-500">Every box is a real class in the repo. <span class="mono">FixedTags</span>, <span class="mono">regopt.Optimize</span> and <span class="mono">DfaMinimizer</span> ride along inside the boxes they optimize — each has its own chapter.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-bold text-zinc-900">Chapters</h2>
        <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
            {
                pages.map((p) => (
                    <a href={p.href} class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-4 transition hover:border-teal-500 hover:shadow-sm">
                        <span class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-300 group-hover:text-teal-600">{p.num}</span>
                        <span class="text-sm font-medium text-zinc-800 group-hover:text-teal-800">{p.title}</span>
                    </a>
                ))
            }
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-500">
            Seven chapters contain interactive playgrounds — a naive backtracker with a visible step count, a Pike-VM thread simulator, a subset-construction stepper, a live register file, and more. Everything that runs in your browser is a faithful miniature of the corresponding Java code, with the file and class names given so you can go read the real thing.
        </p>
    </section>
</Base>
